--- !u!114 &883845876
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 883845874}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 4027ffe91b9e07548aad7b8a68738e42, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::RocketTimeline.UI.TopicFactsPagerUI
  root: {fileID: 883845874}
  titleText: {fileID: 1336277246}
  subtitleText: {fileID: 1394181043}
  topicIcon: {fileID: 1051732008}
  headingTextA: {fileID: 483558041}
  bodyTextA: {fileID: 828263462}
  blockBRoot: {fileID: 1145757113}
  blockBSpacerRoot: {fileID: 1105967485}
  headingTextB: {fileID: 1596871145}
  bodyTextB: {fileID: 622692831}
  contentScrollRect: {fileID: 1681989820}
  pageIndicatorText: {fileID: 964683923}
  facts:
  - heading: 'The Lunar Rover: A Moon Car'
    body: The Apollo Lunar Roving Vehicle was a 4-wheeled, 2-seat "moon buggy" with
      a top speed only about as fast as a human can run - astronauts drove it up
      to 20 km (12 mi) at a time, exploring far beyond walking range near the landing
      site. Apollo 15 in 1971 was the fourth crewed Moon landing and the first to
      carry one, and astronaut Jim Irwin can be seen loading gear onto it in the
      mission's own photographs.
  - heading: Lunokhod 1
    body: The Soviet Union's Lunokhod 1 finished its mission in 1971 as the first
      remote-controlled rover ever to operate on the Moon, driven entirely from a
      control room back on Earth rather than carrying a crew. It proved that a robotic
      vehicle could survive and keep working on another world long enough to be genuinely
      useful - a lesson every later planetary rover, on the Moon or on Mars, has
      built on since.
  - heading: 'Mariner 2: First to Another Planet'
    body: Launched in July 1962, Mariner 2 became the first successful probe to reach
      another planet, flying past Venus at 34,838 km (21,648 mi) on 14 December 1962
      and scanning it for just 42 minutes. In that brief window it revealed cool
      clouds sitting over a surface of at least 425 degC (800 degF) - proof that
      Venus's thick atmosphere hides a genuinely hellish world underneath, not the
      tropical twin of Earth some had imagined.
  - heading: 'Pioneer 10: Fastest Spacecraft Ever Built'
    body: Launched in March 1972, Pioneer 10 was the first spacecraft to cross the
      asteroid belt and the first to reach Jupiter. At launch it was the fastest
      spacecraft ever built, 51,670 km/h (32,107 mph), and it held the title of most
      remote human-made object until Voyager 1 overtook it in 1998. Its last signal
      arrived in 2003, and it is still drifting silently toward the star Aldebaran
      - due to arrive in over 2 million years.
  - heading: 'Mariner 10: The First Gravity Assist'
    body: In 1974, Mariner 10 became the first spacecraft to visit Mercury - and
      the first ever to use a gravity assist, swinging past Venus on 5 February 1974
      to bend its path onward toward the Sun. Across three separate Mercury flybys
      it sent back 12,000 photographs of a heavily cratered, Moon-like world, though
      its flight path happened to photograph the same side each time, leaving part
      of the planet completely unmapped.
  - heading: 'Vega 1 & 2: Balloons at Venus'
    body: Launched by the Soviet Union in December 1984, Vega 1 and 2 flew past Venus
      and released landers plus instrument packages riding Teflon-coated balloons
      into the thick atmosphere. The balloons survived about 46 hours, radioing back
      cloud and wind data from an altitude where a human observer would have been
      crushed and cooked within minutes. Both probes then continued onward to fly
      past Halley's Comet in March 1986, turning one mission into two entirely different
      discoveries.
  - heading: 'Viking 1: First to Live on Mars'
    body: NASA's Viking 1, launched in 1975, became the first spacecraft to land
      on and explore the surface of Mars in 1976, returning high-resolution photographs
      and sampling the Martian soil directly in search of signs of life. Its companion,
      Viking 2, landed weeks later on the opposite side of the planet, and together
      the two landers kept transmitting data from the Martian surface for years longer
      than engineers had ever planned for.
  - heading: 'Voyager: The Grand Tour'
    body: Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 and 2 toured the outer planets on a trajectory
      so rare it only lines up once every 176 years. Voyager 2 alone flew past Jupiter
      (1979), Saturn (1981), Uranus (1986), and Neptune (1989) - a single spacecraft
      visiting four worlds by riding each planet's gravity on to the next. Both probes
      are still transmitting today from interstellar space, decades past their original
      mission.
  - heading: 'Surveyor: Paving the Way for Apollo'
    body: NASA's Surveyor program soft-landed seven robotic spacecraft on the Moon
      in the late 1960s, returning 87,674 photographs between them and proving the
      landing techniques - and scouting the terrain - that the crewed Apollo missions
      would depend on just a few years later. Surveyor 3 even sampled the lunar soil
      directly, and Apollo 12's astronauts later retrieved pieces of it to study
      how two and a half years on the Moon had aged its hardware.
  - heading: 'Modern Mars Rovers: Curiosity and Perseverance'
    body: NASA's car-sized Curiosity rover landed in Gale Crater in August 2012 and
      is still exploring today, while its twin-in-design successor Perseverance launched
      in July 2020 and touched down in February 2021 at Jezero Crater - an ancient
      river delta chosen for its potential to preserve signs of past microbial life.
      Both run on a plutonium power source good for decades rather than fragile solar
      panels, and Perseverance's MOXIE instrument proved astronauts could one day
      make breathable oxygen from the Martian atmosphere itself. It also carried
      Ingenuity, the first aircraft to fly on another planet, which completed 72
      flights before retiring in 2024.
