--- !u!114 &1930163686
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1930163684}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 4027ffe91b9e07548aad7b8a68738e42, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::RocketTimeline.UI.TopicFactsPagerUI
  root: {fileID: 1930163684}
  titleText: {fileID: 1489546967}
  subtitleText: {fileID: 1372548886}
  topicIcon: {fileID: 455744321}
  headingTextA: {fileID: 965837036}
  bodyTextA: {fileID: 1050855173}
  blockBRoot: {fileID: 269621550}
  blockBSpacerRoot: {fileID: 879209775}
  headingTextB: {fileID: 307412173}
  bodyTextB: {fileID: 928885664}
  contentScrollRect: {fileID: 720115817}
  pageIndicatorText: {fileID: 3482810}
  facts:
  - heading: Thousands and Counting
    body: Thousands of satellites have been launched since the Soviet Union's Sputnik
      1 opened the space age in 1957, and the number in orbit keeps climbing every
      year. Most sit in low Earth orbit (LEO), only 200 to 2,000 km (125 to 1,250
      mi) up - close enough that each one completes a full trip around the planet
      in roughly 2 hours, fast enough to cross the night sky in just a few minutes.
  - heading: Four Kinds of Orbit
    body: Low Earth orbit is near and fast, the default choice for crewed missions
      and modern internet constellations. Geostationary orbit sits 35,800 km up above
      the equator with a 24-hour period, so a satellite appears to hang motionless
      over one spot - ideal for TV and weather satellites watching the same patch
      of Earth all day. Polar orbit instead surveys the whole planet strip by strip
      as Earth spins beneath it, while tilted or elliptical orbits suit certain observatories.
  - heading: 'Finding Your Way: GPS, GLONASS, Galileo'
    body: GPS (USA) uses more than 30 satellites spread across 6 orbital planes about
      24,000 km (15,000 mi) up. At any moment 3 to 4 are visible above a given point
      on Earth, but a receiver needs 4 at once to solve its position and correct
      its own clock. Russia runs a similar system, GLONASS, and Europe's Galileo
      constellation had 26 satellites in orbit by December 2021, building toward
      a fleet of 30 steering everything from phone maps to commercial shipping.
  - heading: Watching the Weather
    body: Weather satellites like ESA's Meteosats sit in geostationary orbit at 35,800
      km (22,240 mi), locked to a 24-hour period so their cameras stay fixed on one
      side of Earth and can track the same storm system developing hour after hour.
      That steady vantage point is what makes the swirling cloud-motion footage on
      a nightly weather forecast possible, and it lets meteorologists spot a hurricane
      forming days before it ever reaches land.
  - heading: Eyes on the Ground
    body: Earth observation satellites track land-use change, ocean currents, and
      air pollution, and combining images taken from different angles produces detailed
      3-D terrain data. The sharpest can resolve objects as small as 50 cm (20 in)
      - sharp enough to read a newspaper headline from orbit - and radar satellites
      can image the ground at night or straight through cloud cover, since radar
      waves pass through clouds that would block an ordinary camera entirely.
  - heading: Connecting the World
    body: Telecommunications satellites transformed radio, TV, and phone - the first
      live transatlantic TV relay, from America to Britain, went out in 1962 and
      was watched as something close to a miracle at the time. Modern satellites
      carry hundreds of digital TV channels straight to home dishes, and satellite
      phones give reliable coverage in deserts, oceans, or mountain ranges with no
      ground network at all.
