--- !u!114 &2031205906
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2031205904}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 4027ffe91b9e07548aad7b8a68738e42, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::RocketTimeline.UI.TopicFactsPagerUI
  root: {fileID: 2031205904}
  titleText: {fileID: 1099777536}
  subtitleText: {fileID: 317949564}
  topicIcon: {fileID: 280833671}
  headingTextA: {fileID: 667182216}
  bodyTextA: {fileID: 1977287349}
  blockBRoot: {fileID: 1362494159}
  blockBSpacerRoot: {fileID: 835476534}
  headingTextB: {fileID: 1906601279}
  bodyTextB: {fileID: 2086183770}
  contentScrollRect: {fileID: 1719436813}
  pageIndicatorText: {fileID: 453085219}
  facts:
  - heading: A Truly International Effort
    body: The ISS is a partnership of 15 to 16 nations - the United States, Canada,
      Japan, Russia, and 11 member states of the European Space Agency - making it
      one of the largest cooperative engineering projects in history. Components
      built on different continents had to fit together perfectly on the first try
      in orbit, with no factory nearby to fix a mistake, which makes its record of
      successful assembly even more remarkable.
  - heading: Built Piece by Piece
    body: Construction began in November 1998 when Russia's Proton rocket launched
      Zarya, the station's first module. Russia's Zvezda module followed in December
      1998, providing the first living quarters, and after further assembly flights
      the first 3-person crew arrived by Soyuz for a 5-month stay. The ISS has been
      continuously, permanently inhabited ever since - the longest unbroken human
      presence in space in history.
  - heading: Bigger Than You'd Think
    body: The completed station spans about 109 m (356 ft) across its truss and 88
      m (290 ft) along its modules, with a mass of roughly 420,000 kg (925,000 lb).
      Its pressurized living space totals about 935 cubic meters (33,000 cubic feet)
      - roughly the same volume as a five-bedroom house - all of it assembled 400
      km above Earth from pieces that were each individually launched, docked, and
      bolted together by hand.
  - heading: Solar Power on a Massive Scale
    body: The ISS carries 8 pairs of solar panels, each 73 m (240 ft) long - longer
      than the wingspan of a Boeing 777. Together, more than 262,000 individual solar
      cells can generate up to 110 kW of power, and the whole array rotates continuously
      to track the Sun as the station circles the planet once every 90 minutes, keeping
      the lights, labs, and life-support systems running non-stop.
  - heading: Six Laboratories in Orbit
    body: 'The station''s science comes from 6 main laboratories: a US lab, two Russian
      research modules, Japan''s Experiment Module (with its own external "back porch"
      for experiments directly exposed to space), and ESA''s Columbus Orbital Facility.
      Together they offer 33 International Standard Payload Racks for research, turning
      the ISS into one of the most productive science facilities ever built - one
      that just happens to orbit the Earth.'
  - heading: The Backbone and the Arm
    body: A central US-built Integrated Truss Structure carries the station's main
      solar arrays, which rotate to track the Sun as the ISS orbits. The Canadian-built
      robotic arm system - a 55-foot arm plus the Special Purpose Dexterous Manipulator
      - rides along the truss on its own mobile base, letting crews assemble, maintain,
      and even capture visiting cargo spacecraft from inside the station.
  - heading: A Constant Stream of Visitors
    body: Over the ISS's lifetime, crew and cargo have arrived aboard the piloted
      Space Shuttle and Soyuz, and Russia's uncrewed Progress and Europe's uncrewed
      Automated Transfer Vehicle (ATV) for resupply. An emergency crew-return vehicle
      always stays docked whenever the station is inhabited, ready to bring the entire
      crew home on short notice if anything ever forced an evacuation - a lifeboat
      that has thankfully never had to be used.
  - heading: Science Every Single Day
    body: Crews run experiments daily in human biology, medical research, physical
      sciences, and Earth observation - from growing protein crystals that are impossible
      to grow properly under gravity, to developing new metal alloys that only mix
      evenly in freefall. Hundreds of scientists on the ground design and monitor
      these experiments alongside the crew, turning the station into a laboratory
      that never really closes.
  - heading: A Milestone Spacewalk
    body: In 2019, US astronauts Christina Koch and Jessica Meir completed the first
      all-female spacewalk outside the ISS, stepping out together to replace a failed
      power-regulation device on the station's exterior. The spacewalk had originally
      been planned a few months earlier but was postponed for lack of a second correctly-sized
      spacesuit in orbit - a scheduling hiccup that made headlines in its own right.
  - heading: A New Way to Reach the Station
    body: After the Space Shuttle retired in 2011, NASA depended entirely on Russia's
      Soyuz to ferry astronauts to the ISS for nearly a decade. That changed in May
      2020, when SpaceX's Crew Dragon carried astronauts Bob Behnken and Doug Hurley
      to the station on the Demo-2 test flight - the first crewed orbital launch
      from American soil since the Shuttle era, developed under NASA's Commercial
      Crew Program. Crew Dragon has flown every NASA crew rotation since, while Boeing's
      Starliner, developed alongside it, is still working toward its own first operational
      flight.
